<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kailasa temple, Ellora</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Kailasanatha temple, Ellora</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kailasa temple at Ellora in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> carved downward from a single mass of basalt rock. Commissioned by the Rashtrakuta king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> I in the 8th century, it reproduces a free-standing temple complex with shrine, halls, gateway and life-size elephants, and is the largest monolithic rock-cut structure in the world. It is the centrepiece of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellora-caves">Ellora Caves</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kailasa-temple-ellora.jpg" alt="Kailasa temple, Ellora" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kailash_temple_(Ellora_cave_no_15)_at_Verul.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kailasa temple, also called Cave 16, is the largest of the 34 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, Buddhist and Jain cave temples and monasteries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellora-caves">Ellora Caves</a>, which stretch for over two kilometres along a sloping basalt cliff. It has no dedicatory inscription, but it was certainly commissioned by a Rashtrakuta ruler. Most scholars attribute it to Krishna I, who reigned from about 756 to 773 CE, on the strength of two grants.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vadodara">Vadodara</a> copper-plate of about 812-813 CE names Krishnaraja as patron of the Kailasanatha temple, and the Kadaba grant seems to credit him likewise. The attribution is not completely certain, because neither inscription is physically connected to the caves. Hermann Goetz argued that work began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga">Dantidurga</a> and continued under later kings, while M. K. Dhavalikar concluded that the main part was finished under Krishna I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple was carved from the top downward out of the rock face, and archaeologists believe it was sculpted from a single rock. The top of the structure over the sanctuary stands 32.6 metres above the court below. A two-storeyed gateway opens into a U-shaped courtyard measuring 82 metres by 46 metres at the base, edged by a columned arcade three storeys high with large sculpted panels. Within it stand the central shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, with a Dravidian shikhara and a flat-roofed mandapa on 16 pillars, and an image of Nandi, his bull.</p><p>Five detached shrines include three to the river goddesses Ganga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a>. The base of the hall shows scenes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, and one of its best-known sculptures shows Ravana attempting to lift Mount Kailasa. Its style shows Chalukya and Pallava influence, and the main shrine resembles the Virupaksha Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pattadakal">Pattadakal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>A medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> legend, first recorded in the Katha-Kalpataru of Krishna Yajnavalki, tells of a queen who vowed to fast until she saw the top of a temple, and of an architect named Kokasa who carved the shikhara first and finished it within a week. Dhavalikar suggests that Kokasa was the chief architect and that the temple was first called Manikeshwar. Dhavalikar also estimated that 250 labourers could have cut the temple in about five and a half years. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> historian Rafi al-Din Shirazi, the Kailasanatha was an ancient wonder, and the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> called it a marvellous place for strolling.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kailasa Temple, Ellora” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chalukya builders drew on the Pallava Kailasanatha temple at Kanchipuram, and their work in turn shaped the Rashtrakuta dynasty, whose rock-cut <b>Kailasa temple</b> at Ellora is often compared with the Virupaksha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A grant of 812 praises Krishna I for a marvellous temple at Elapura, generally identified with the <b>Kailasa temple</b> at Ellora, cut downwards into the basalt hillside as a single monolithic shrine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
